      "learning_objective_id": "CN_T01_1_LO02",
      "question_category": "misconception_analysis",
      "stem": "Early ARPANET used a single host-to-host protocol (NCP) that assumed one underlying network prevented packet loss. Which broad architectural shift enabled diverse networks (such as packet radio, satellite, and local Ethernet networks) to interconnect into the global Internet?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Replacing every local wired and wireless link worldwide with one uniform physical circuit technology."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Moving all per-flow connection state into intermediate routers and forbidding end hosts from retransmitting lost packets."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Adopting open-architecture internetworking (with TCP/IP) so independent networks connect via best-effort routers while end hosts handle end-to-end reliability."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Keeping a single central host-name table file on one machine for all connected hosts instead of distributed naming."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "As described in the Internet Society's Brief History of the Internet ('The Initial Internetting Concepts') and RFC 1958 Section 2, open-architecture networking lets each network choose its own internal technology while stateless gateways/routers forward best-effort packets across networks and end hosts implement end-to-end reliability (TCP/IP).",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Open-architecture networking explicitly avoids forcing all networks onto one physical technology; diverse networks interwork as peers through an internetworking layer.",
        "B": "In the Internet architecture (RFC 1122 §1.1.2, RFC 1958 §2, ISOC Concepts), intermediate gateways/routers keep no per-flow connection state and end hosts handle retransmission/reliability.",
        "D": "As host counts grew, a single central host table could not scale and was replaced by the distributed Domain Name System (DNS)."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "open_architecture_vs_uniform_hardware",
        "stateless_core_vs_stateful_routers",
        "central_table_vs_dns"
      ],
