      "correct_option_id": "A",
      "answer_explanation": "Per RFC 1122 Section 1.1.1, a host (end system) executes application programs at the network edge—covering both client devices (`Laptop L1`, `Desktop PC`) and servers (`Cloud Web Server`). As RFC 1122 Section 1.1.3 and RFC 1958 Section 2.2 explain, hosts interface to diverse physical/wireless link-layer media (Wi-Fi, copper Ethernet, and fiber), while `Edge Router / AP` and `ISP Core Router` act as packet-switching gateways/routers interconnecting networks.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "B": "`Edge Router / AP` acts as a packet-switching gateway/router interconnecting the local wireless/wired links to the fiber uplink, whereas `Cloud Web Server` is an application-running end-system host at the edge.",
        "C": "Both wireless radio channels (such as Wi-Fi) and wired copper/fiber cables are link-layer transmission media connecting devices, not end-system hosts.",
        "D": "Forwarding packets between multiple interconnected links is the defining role of a gateway/IP router in the core, not an end-system host."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "easy",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "edge_vs_core_confusion",
        "wireless_not_a_link",
        "router_as_host"
      ],
