      "learning_objective_id": "CN_T01_1_LO02",
      "question_category": "short_application",
      "stem": "When early researchers connected remote time-shared computers over a dial-up telephone line, they observed that interactive computer traffic came in short bursts separated by idle pauses, making the circuit-switched telephone network poorly suited for computer communications. Why did moving to **packet switching** solve this limitation?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Packet switching reserves an exclusive end-to-end synchronous bit circuit for every user even during idle pauses."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Packet switching removes all destination addresses so routers forward traffic without inspecting headers."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Splitting messages into packets lets multiple users and flows share communication links dynamically instead of tying up an idle circuit."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Packet switching forces all connected computers to run the same single hardware clock speed on one shared cable."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "As documented in the Internet Society's Brief History of the Internet ('Origins of the Internet' and 'The Initial Internetting Concepts'), the TX-2 to Q-32 dial-up test confirmed that circuit-switched telephone links were inadequate for bursty computer interaction, whereas packet switching enabled efficient statistical sharing of network links across users.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Reserving a synchronous end-to-end bit path regardless of idle periods describes traditional circuit switching, the model packet switching replaced for computer data.",
        "B": "Packet-switched gateways explicitly interpret packet IP headers for global addressing and forwarding rather than omitting destination addresses.",
        "D": "Packet-switched networks accommodate wide differences in link speeds and technologies (RFC 1958 §2.2, ISOC Concepts) rather than requiring one hardware clock speed."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "easy",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "packet_vs_circuit_sharing",
        "headerless_packets",
        "uniform_clock_myth"
      ],
      "authorship_type": "AI-assisted team-authored",
      "reference_sources": [
        "https://www.internetsociety.org/internet/history-internet/brief-history-internet/#Origins",
        "https://www.internetsociety.org/internet/history-internet/brief-history-internet/#Concepts",
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1958.html#section-2"
      ],
      "verification_method": "source_cross_check",
