      "question_category": "short_application",
      "stem": "A student's laptop sends a message over a home Wi-Fi link, across an ISP optical fiber link, and onto a university wired Ethernet LAN. Which architectural principle from the Internet's evolution allows these diverse wired and wireless technologies to interoperate seamlessly?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Converting every Wi-Fi access point and fiber trunk globally to use identical physical copper Ethernet signaling."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Using a hardware-independent internetworking protocol (IP) above diverse local link-layer protocols so each network handles its own medium while exchanging common IP datagrams."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Requiring application developers to write separate versions of web and email software for Wi-Fi, fiber, and copper links."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Mandating that only one single network provider owns every link between the laptop and the university server."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "B",
      "answer_explanation": "RFC 1958 Section 2.2 states that the Internet-level protocol must be independent of the hardware medium and hardware addressing so it can interconnect fundamentally different transmission media. RFC 1122 Section 1.1.3 similarly notes a wide variety of link-layer protocols beneath the common Internet layer.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Open-architecture networking (ISOC Concepts) leaves each individual network free to choose its own internal physical/link technology rather than forcing one medium.",
        "C": "Decoupling IP addressing and forwarding from underlying link hardware lets applications run on top of a single Internet platform across heterogeneous media (RFC 1958 §2.2).",
        "D": "The Internet interconnects independently operated providers and networks ('Transition to Widespread Infrastructure'), not one monolithic single-owner network."
      },
