      "topic_id": "CN_T01_1",
      "learning_objective_id": "CN_T01_1_LO02",
      "question_category": "misconception_analysis",
      "stem": "A student writes two claims about the architectural evolution of the Internet:\n1) \"A single central operations center owns the Internet, approves every packet flow, and stores all host names in one master host-table file.\"\n2) \"Legacy fixed Class A, B, and C network sizes were replaced by classless prefix addressing (CIDR) so address blocks could match organizational sizes more flexibly and help control router table growth.\"\n\nWhich evaluation of these two claims is accurate?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Both claims are accurate because global internetworking requires one central authority to route packets and enforce fixed Class A/B/C boundaries."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Claim (1) is accurate because DNS is a single central file on one server, whereas Claim (2) is inaccurate because IPv4 still assigns only fixed Class A/B/C sizes."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Both claims are inaccurate because early networks never outgrew a single host table and never faced routing-table scaling pressure."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Claim (1) is inaccurate because the Internet has no single owner or centralized operational control and replaced the single host table with distributed DNS; Claim (2) is accurate because CIDR introduced flexible prefix blocks and aggregation."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "RFC 1958 Section 2.4 states that nobody owns the Internet and there is no centralized control (matching ISOC's 'no global control at the operations level'), and ISOC 'Proving the Ideas' explains that a single host table could not scale and was replaced by distributed DNS. Meanwhile, ISOC 'Proving the Ideas' and RFC 4632 Section 3.1 confirm that CIDR deprecated fixed Class A/B/C networks in favor of variable-length prefixes to fit site needs and control routing table growth.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "The Internet operates without centralized control (RFC 1958 §2.4, ISOC Concepts) and deprecated Class A/B/C assignment in favor of CIDR (RFC 4632 §3.1).",
        "B": "DNS is a scalable distributed mechanism rather than one central host table (ISOC 'Proving the Ideas'), and CIDR replaced fixed Class A/B/C network classes (RFC 4632 §3.1).",
        "C": "Growth of LANs and hosts explicitly rendered a single host table unfeasible and stressed router addressing tables, motivating DNS and CIDR (ISOC 'Proving the Ideas')."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "central_internet_control_myth",
        "dns_vs_single_host_table",
        "classful_vs_cidr_evolution"
      ],
      "authorship_type": "AI-assisted team-authored",
      "reference_sources": [
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1958.html#section-2",
        "https://www.internetsociety.org/internet/history-internet/brief-history-internet/#Concepts",
        "https://www.internetsociety.org/internet/history-internet/brief-history-internet/#Proving-the-Ideas",
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4632.html#section-3.1"
      ],
