      "topic_id": "CN_T01_3",
      "learning_objective_id": "CN_T01_3_LO01",
      "question_category": "short_application",
      "stem": "Users in a campus lab browse webpages in short bursts: each user downloads a page for 1 second and then spends 9 seconds reading while sending nothing. Why does **packet switching** use a shared core link more efficiently than **synchronous time-division circuit switching (STDM)** for this traffic?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Packet switching speeds up the physical speed of light inside the cable during reading pauses."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Packet switching forces all users to read webpages at the exact same speed so no pauses occur."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "STDM cannot carry text or web pages unless each user owns a dedicated optical fiber cable."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Under STDM, each idle user's reserved time slot goes unused during the 9-second reading pause, whereas packet switching lets active users transmit on demand during those idle intervals."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "With **STDM circuit reservation**, a user's fixed time slot stays empty whenever that user is not sending—wasting capacity for 9 out of 10 seconds here. With **packet switching (statistical multiplexing)**, links are allocated on demand per packet, so other active users can immediately use link capacity while someone is reading.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Signal propagation speed is fixed by the physical medium, not altered by switching discipline.",
        "B": "Users continue to generate bursty traffic independently; packet switching adapts to idle periods rather than synchronizing human reading times.",
        "C": "STDM can carry arbitrary bit streams over a shared physical link by dividing time into fixed quanta; its inefficiency for bursty data comes from wasted idle slots, not inability to carry data."
      },
