      "topic_id": "CN_T01_3",
      "learning_objective_id": "CN_T01_3_LO01",
      "question_category": "short_application",
      "stem": "Four hosts share a single `10 Mbps` outgoing link through a packet switch using **statistical multiplexing** (no per-host circuit reservation). At 10:00, only Host 1 sends a file while Hosts 2–4 are idle. At 10:05, all four hosts simultaneously send traffic totaling `16 Mbps` toward that `10 Mbps` link. Which pair of outcomes matches packet-switched behavior?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "At 10:00, Host 1 can use up to the full `10 Mbps` link; at 10:05, excess arriving packets are buffered in switch memory and may be dropped if the buffer fills."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "At 10:00, Host 1 is capped at `2.5 Mbps` because `3/4` of the link stays reserved for Hosts 2–4; at 10:05, all `16 Mbps` passes with zero buffering."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "At 10:00, Host 1 can use up to the full `10 Mbps`; at 10:05, the packet switch temporarily increases the outgoing link's physical capacity to `16 Mbps`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "At 10:00, Host 1 cannot send until Hosts 2–4 also transmit; at 10:05, the switch blocks Host 4 from establishing a call."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "A",
      "answer_explanation": "Because statistical multiplexing allocates the link **on demand** without fixed `2.5 Mbps` reservations, Host 1 can transmit packets back-to-back at up to `10 Mbps` when Hosts 2–4 are idle. When combined arrivals (`16 Mbps`) exceed the `10 Mbps` link capacity, the switch buffers excess packets in memory and drops packets if the buffer runs out of space (congestion).",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "B": "Capping Host 1 at `2.5 Mbps` describes static circuit/slot division (like STDM), not packet sharing; also, `16 Mbps` cannot pass through a `10 Mbps` link simultaneously without buffering or loss.",
        "C": "A switch cannot exceed the physical link rate (`10 Mbps`) on the wire; excess arrivals above `10 Mbps` must wait in a buffer or be dropped.",
        "D": "Packet-switched hosts send independently on demand without waiting for other hosts or performing circuit-call admission."
      },
