      "question_category": "short_application",
      "stem": "An administrator splits `192.168.40.0/24` into **4 equal-sized ordinary IPv4 subnets** (reserving the all-zeros network and all-ones broadcast addresses in each subnet). What are the resulting prefix length, subnet mask, usable host count per subnet, and the network, usable host range, and broadcast address of the **3rd subnet** in numerical order?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "/26 (255.255.255.192), 62 usable hosts each; 3rd subnet: Network 192.168.40.192, Usable 192.168.40.193–192.168.40.254, Broadcast 192.168.40.255."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "/28 (255.255.255.240), 14 usable hosts each; 3rd subnet: Network 192.168.40.32, Usable 192.168.40.33–192.168.40.46, Broadcast 192.168.40.47."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "/26 (255.255.255.192), 62 usable hosts each; 3rd subnet: Network 192.168.40.128, Usable 192.168.40.129–192.168.40.190, Broadcast 192.168.40.191."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "/26 (255.255.255.192), 64 usable hosts each; 3rd subnet: Network 192.168.40.128, Usable 192.168.40.128–192.168.40.191, Broadcast 192.168.40.192."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "Splitting a `/24` (`256` addresses) into $4 = 2^2$ equal subnets extends the prefix by `2` bits to `/26` (mask `255.255.255.192`), leaving $h = 6$ host bits ($2^6 = 64$ addresses/block, $64 - 2 = 62$ usable hosts per subnet). The 4 subnets are `.0/26` (`0`–`63`), `.64/26` (`64`–`127`), `.128/26` (`128`–`191`), and `.192/26` (`192`–`255`). Thus the 3rd subnet is `192.168.40.128/26`, usable `192.168.40.129`–`192.168.40.190`, broadcast `192.168.40.191`.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "`192.168.40.192/26` (`192`–`255`) is the **4th** subnet, not the 3rd (`192.168.40.128/26`).",
        "B": "Borrowing `4` bits (`/28`, mask `255.255.255.240`) splits a `/24` into `16` subnets of `16` addresses (`14` usable) rather than `4` equal subnets.",
        "D": "Each `/26` has `62` usable hosts (not `64`), `.128` and `.191` are reserved network/broadcast addresses, and `.192` starts the 4th subnet."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
