      "question_family_id": "QF_CN_T03_1_F07",
      "concept_id": "CN_C03",
      "topic_id": "CN_T03_1",
      "learning_objective_id": "CN_T03_1_LO02",
      "question_category": "short_application",
      "stem": "Client host `198.51.100.5` runs two separate client programs at the same time—App 1 using local source port `51001` and App 2 using local source port `51002`—both querying the same server service at `(203.0.113.20, 8080)`. When the server sends two replies back to `198.51.100.5`, how does `198.51.100.5` deliver each reply to the right client program?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "The server stamps App 1's and App 2's host-OS process IDs into the host address field."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Each reply addresses destination `(198.51.100.5, 51001)` or `(198.51.100.5, 51002)` using the client's source port from the request, so `198.51.100.5` demultiplexes by destination port."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "`198.51.100.5` cannot tell the replies apart because both came from server port `8080`, so both replies go to App 1."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "The server sends both replies to destination port `8080` on `198.51.100.5`, and App 1 and App 2 take turns reading them."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "B",
      "answer_explanation": "As `Systems Approach` §5.1 and RFC 768 explain, the server knows each client's source port (`51001` or `51002`) from the incoming request header and addresses its reply to that port on `198.51.100.5`. Combining `(host address, port)` lets `198.51.100.5` demultiplex each reply to App 1 or App 2.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Transport protocols use abstract **ports** rather than OS process IDs across heterogeneous operating systems, and process IDs are not placed in the host address field.",
        "C": "Even though both replies share source `(203.0.113.20, 8080)`, they carry distinct destination ports (`51001` vs. `51002`) on `198.51.100.5`.",
        "D": "Port `8080` is the server's listening port on `203.0.113.20`; replies return to the client's source ports (`51001` and `51002`), not `8080`."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "client_source_port_reply",
        "concurrent_client_demux"
      ],
      "authorship_type": "AI-assisted team-authored",
      "reference_sources": [
        "https://book.systemsapproach.org/e2e/udp.html#simple-demultiplexor-udp",
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6335.html#section-3",
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc768.html#page-2"
      ],
      "verification_method": "source_cross_check",
      "agent_check_status": "draft",
      "human_review_status": "pending",
      "approved_by": null,
      "approved_at": null,
