      "stem": "A sending process passes two separate messages to UDP—first a **40-byte** message `M1`, then a **60-byte** message `M2`—addressed to the same destination `(host, port)`. Assume both datagrams reach the receiving application intact, its port queue does not overflow, and each receive buffer holds at least 60 bytes. What does UDP guarantee when the receiving application reads from that port?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "UDP merges `M1` and `M2` into one 100-byte stream, so the receiver gets 100 bytes and must find the end of `M1` itself."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "UDP preserves boundaries: `M1` arrives as a 40-byte and `M2` as a 60-byte datagram, but `M1` is not guaranteed to arrive first."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "UDP guarantees `M1` is delivered before `M2`, but may split `M2` into two 30-byte datagrams so that it fits the receive buffer."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "UDP delivers only the first datagram queued for the port and discards `M2`, because a UDP port queue can hold just one datagram."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "B",
      "answer_explanation": "Per RFC 768 (p. 2) and `Systems Approach §5.1`, each UDP datagram has an explicit `Length` and is queued/received as a distinct message (preserving the 40-byte and 60-byte boundaries). However, per RFC 8085 §3.3, UDP provides no ordering guarantee, so `M2` could arrive before `M1`.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Merging separate writes into an unstructured byte stream is TCP behavior, not UDP.",
        "C": "UDP delivers each datagram intact rather than splitting `M2`, and it does *not* guarantee that `M1` arrives before `M2`.",
        "D": "The receiver queues arriving messages for a port; it is not limited by UDP to one datagram. The question excludes queue overflow."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "easy",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "datagram_boundaries_preserved",
        "udp_reordering_possible"
      ],
      "authorship_type": "AI-assisted team-authored",
      "reference_sources": [
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc768.html#page-2",
        "https://book.systemsapproach.org/e2e/udp.html#simple-demultiplexor-udp",
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8085.html#section-3.3"
      ],
      "verification_method": "source_cross_check",
