      "concept_id": "CN_C05",
      "topic_id": "CN_T05_2",
      "learning_objective_id": "CN_T05_2_LO02",
      "question_category": "misconception_analysis",
      "stem": "A developer writes two separate `200-byte` JSON objects over TCP using two `SEND` calls and sets TCP's `PUSH` flag on each call, arguing:\n\"Setting the `PUSH` flag forces TCP to preserve record boundaries so each `RECEIVE` call on the peer will return exactly one `200-byte` JSON object.\"\n\nWhich explanation identifies the flaw per RFC 9293?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "TCP ignores `SEND` calls smaller than `1,500` bytes unless the application disables byte ordering first."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "The `PUSH` flag forces the receiver to discard the first JSON object whenever a second `SEND` arrives, so only one object is ever read."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "TCP automatically inserts a `\\n` delimiter whenever `PUSH` is set, so only binary data would lack boundaries, not these JSON objects."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "RFC 9293 says `PSH` is not a record marker, so successive sends may still be coalesced or split across the peer's `RECEIVE` calls."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "RFC 9293 §3.9.1.2 explicitly states: *\"The PSH bit is not a record marker and is independent of segment boundaries.\"* While `PUSH` asks TCP to transmit/deliver buffered data promptly, RFC 9293 §3.9.1.2–3.9.1.3 confirms that `PSH` does not delimit application messages, and a receiver's `RECEIVE` buffer may be filled across partial or combined segments—so applications must still implement explicit message framing.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "TCP transmits data of any byte count and never disables byte ordering.",
        "B": "`PUSH` asks for prompt delivery of queued bytes; it never discards prior data.",
        "C": "TCP never injects `\\n` or any other delimiter bytes into the user's byte stream."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "psh_not_record_marker",
        "one_send_equals_one_recv_myth"
      ],
      "authorship_type": "AI-assisted team-authored",
      "reference_sources": [
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293.html#section-3.9.1.2",
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293.html#section-3.9.1.3"
      ],
      "verification_method": "source_cross_check",
      "agent_check_status": "draft",
      "human_review_status": "pending",
