      "stem": "Consider two candidate paths from Source `S` to Destination `D` with explicit link costs:\n- **Path X (`S -> R1 -> D`):** link `S–R1` has cost `2`, link `R1–D` has cost `4`.\n- **Path Y (`S -> R2 -> D`):** link `S–R2` has cost `3`, link `R2–D` has cost `2`.\n- **Rule:** Choose the path with the lower total sum of link costs.\n\nWhich path is selected from `S` to `D`?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Path X (S -> R1 -> D), because link S–R1 has the smallest single-hop cost of 2"
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Either path at cost 2, because only the final hop's cost counts toward the route"
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "No path can be chosen, because neither path starts and ends with identical costs"
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Path Y (S -> R2 -> D), because its total summed path cost is 5 compared with Path X's total cost of 6"
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "Summing link costs gives Path X = 2 + 4 = 6 and Path Y = 3 + 2 = 5, so Path Y has the lower total cost.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Greedy selection on the first hop ignores the second hop (cost 4), giving Path X a higher total cost of 6.",
        "B": "Total path cost sums every link along the path (3 + 2 = 5 for Path Y), not just one link's cost.",
        "C": "Candidate paths only need comparable total sums under the stated rule; individual hops do not have to match."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "first_hop_cost_vs_total_cost",
        "last_hop_only_cost_myth"
      ],
