      "title_en": "A22 Wireshark Capture Analysis Lab: Display Filters, Dissected Fields, and Capture-Scope Limits",
      "title_vi": "Thực hành A22 Phân tích gói tin Wireshark: Bộ lọc hiển thị, Trường giao thức và Giới hạn bản bắt gói",
      "activity_type": "packet_capture_analysis",
      "problem_statement": "Analyze the supplied 10-frame synthetic local lab packet summary fixture (`synthetic_fixture`) as if opened in Wireshark (or reproduce an equivalent local loopback/lab-LAN capture on your own authorized test machine — never capture live traffic on third-party networks):\n\nSynthetic Local Lab Capture Fixture (`synthetic_fixture`, 10 frames, Ethernet + IPv4 + UDP/TCP/ARP/ICMP):\n- Frame 1 | t=0.000s | 192.168.10.10:51420 -> 192.168.10.2:53 | DNS | Standard query 0x1a2b A lab.example.test (UDP len=42)\n- Frame 2 | t=0.004s | 192.168.10.2:53 -> 192.168.10.10:51420 | DNS | Standard query response 0x1a2b A lab.example.test -> 192.168.20.20 (TTL=60)\n- Frame 3 | t=0.006s | 192.168.10.10:52310 -> 192.168.20.20:80 | TCP | 52310 -> 80 [SYN] Seq=0 Win=64240 MSS=1460\n- Frame 4 | t=0.009s | 192.168.20.20:80 -> 192.168.10.10:52310 | TCP | 80 -> 52310 [SYN, ACK] Seq=0 Ack=1 Win=65160 MSS=1460\n- Frame 5 | t=0.010s | 192.168.10.10:52310 -> 192.168.20.20:80 | TCP | 52310 -> 80 [ACK] Seq=1 Ack=1 Win=64240\n- Frame 6 | t=0.011s | 192.168.10.10:52310 -> 192.168.20.20:80 | HTTP | GET /index.html HTTP/1.1 (Host: lab.example.test)\n- Frame 7 | t=0.015s | 192.168.20.20:80 -> 192.168.10.10:52310 | HTTP | HTTP/1.1 200 OK (Content-Type: text/html; Content-Length: 128)\n- Frame 8 | t=0.020s | 00:aa:bb:cc:dd:10 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff | ARP | Who has 192.168.10.1? Tell 192.168.10.10\n- Frame 9 | t=0.021s | 00:11:22:33:44:01 -> 00:aa:bb:cc:dd:10 | ARP | 192.168.10.1 is at 00:11:22:33:44:01\n- Frame 10 | t=0.030s | 192.168.10.10 -> 192.168.20.20 | ICMP | Echo (ping) request id=0x0001, seq=1 (capture ends at t=0.031s; no reply in file)\n\n**Tasks:**\n1. **Display Filter Matching (`CN_T09_1_LO01`)**: Determine which frame numbers match each display filter: (a) `dns`, (b) `http`, and (c) `tcp.port == 80`, and explain why `(b)` and `(c)` differ.\n2. **Field Observations (`CN_T09_1_LO01`)**: Record the queried domain name, DNS answer IPv4 address and TTL, client ephemeral TCP port, HTTP request method/URI, and HTTP response status code.\n3. **Capture-Scope Limit Questions (`CN_T09_1_LO02`)**:\n   - Q1: After applying display filter `http`, are Frames 1–5 and 8–10 deleted from the capture file, and does `http` show everything happening on the network?\n   - Q2: Frame 10 shows an ICMP Echo Request with no Echo Reply in this file. Does this prove host `192.168.20.20` is powered off?\n   - Q3: If Wireshark captures only on `PC1`'s NIC in a switched Ethernet LAN without port mirroring (SPAN), which unicast frames between two other hosts `PC2` and `S1` are missing from the capture?",
      "input_spec": "The 10-frame annotated `synthetic_fixture` packet table (or an authorized local lab `.pcapng` capture containing DNS, TCP, HTTP, ARP, and ICMP frames).",
      "output_spec": "Matched frame lists for `dns`, `http`, `tcp.port == 80`; exact extracted protocol fields; and evidence-bounded answers to Q1–Q3 stating capture-scope limitations.",
      "constraints": "Do NOT treat filtered display results as the complete network; do NOT perform live captures on third-party networks; do NOT infer uncaptured host/firewall state beyond what packets prove.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "input": "Display filter: dns",
          "output": "Matches Frames 1 and 2; dns.qry.name = 'lab.example.test', resolved IP = 192.168.20.20"
        },
        {
          "input": "Compare display filter `http` vs `tcp.port == 80`",
          "output": "`http` matches Frames 6–7 (HTTP PDUs only); `tcp.port == 80` matches Frames 3–7 (includes TCP 3-way handshake SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK)."
        }
      ],
      "reference_solution": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "content": "### 1. Display Filter Matches\n- **`dns`**: Frames **1, 2** (UDP port 53 query and response).\n- **`http`**: Frames **6, 7** (only frames containing an HTTP protocol payload).\n- **`tcp.port == 80`**: Frames **3, 4, 5, 6, 7** (matches any TCP segment whose source or destination port is 80, including the TCP 3-way handshake in Frames 3–5 as well as HTTP Frames 6–7).\n\n### 2. Expected Field Observations\n- **DNS Query Name**: `lab.example.test` (Frame 1, Type `A`, Transaction ID `0x1a2b`, client UDP src port `51420` -> resolver `192.168.10.2:53`).\n- **DNS Answer**: `192.168.20.20` with `TTL = 60` seconds (Frame 2).\n- **TCP Transport**: Client `192.168.10.10` uses ephemeral port `52310` to server `192.168.20.20:80` (Frames 3–5 handshake).\n- **HTTP Exchange**: Request `GET /index.html HTTP/1.1` with `Host: lab.example.test` (Frame 6); Response `HTTP/1.1 200 OK`, `Content-Type: text/html`, `Content-Length: 128` (Frame 7).\n\n### 3. Capture-Scope Limit Answers\n- **Q1 (Display filter vs. capture completeness)**: Applying display filter `http` only hides non-matching frames from the Wireshark Packet List view (unlike a capture filter, which drops packets at capture time). Hidden packets remain in the file, and the display view never proves no other traffic existed.\n- **Q2 (Unanswered ICMP Echo Request)**: **No.** Frame 10 proves only that an Echo Request was sent at `t=0.030s` and no reply was captured before `t=0.031s`. In fact, `192.168.20.20` already answered HTTP port 80 at `t=0.015s`; an ICMP reply may be blocked/rate-limited by a firewall or simply occurred after capture stop.\n- **Q3 (Switched-LAN visibility)**: On a switched Ethernet LAN without port mirroring (SPAN) or an inline tap, a single host NIC receives broadcasts (like Frame 8 ARP), multicasts, and unicast frames destined to/from its own MAC — unicast frames exchanged directly between two other switch ports (`PC2` <-> `S1`) are not forwarded to `PC1` and will not appear in the capture."
      },
